; ======================================================================
;  hw_board_q.pi4 - the Arduino UNO Q backend for the HwBoard* seam.
;
;  Three facts the payload ABI (Anvil/Hal/abi.pbi) needs and no command
;  family does: what this board is called, how to reset it, and what state
;  its MMU and caches are in. They have no #CAP_ flag for the same reason
;  HwAddr* has none - every board has an identity, a way to reset and a
;  cache state, so there is nothing that could be absent.
;
;  INCLUDE ORDER: after ArduinoQ/Board/qefi_q.unoq (QEfiReset) and
;  qcon_q.pi4 (UartDrain), and before Anvil/Hal/abi.pbi, which calls all
;  three. House rule 10: this file includes nothing itself.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i HwBoardName()
  ProcedureReturn "Arduino UNO Q (Qualcomm QCM2290)"
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwReboot() - reset the board. Does not return.
;
;  THROUGH UEFI'S RUNTIME SERVICES AND NOT THROUGH A REGISTER. Anvil is a
;  UEFI application here, boot services are still up, and the firmware
;  owns the PMIC. Poking a reset register underneath a firmware that
;  believes it still owns the machine is exactly the class of thing this
;  board's address-safety seam exists to refuse.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwReboot()
  UartDrain()
  QEfiReset()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMmuState() - bit 0 MMU on, bit 1 D-cache on, bit 2 I-cache on.
;
;  ALL THREE ARE 1 HERE AND THE ANSWER IS NOT A GUESS: UEFI runs this
;  application at EL1 with the MMU and both caches already enabled - that
;  is what makes boot services usable at all - and nothing in this image
;  changes any of them.
;
;  IT IS A CONSTANT RATHER THAN A READ OF SCTLR_EL1, and that is the one
;  thing worth saying out loud. The Pi 4 backend reads its register
;  because Anvil there is the thing that turns caches on and off, so the
;  register is the only honest source. Here Anvil never touches them, so
;  a read would return the same three bits every time and would look like
;  a measurement while being a restatement. If this image ever gains a
;  path that disables a cache, this becomes a read of SCTLR_EL1 - and the
;  comment is here so that whoever writes that path knows to.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMmuState()
  ProcedureReturn 7
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMicros() - monotonic microseconds since Anvil started.
;
;  COMPUTED FROM THE ARCHITECTURAL COUNTER AND ITS MEASURED FREQUENCY,
;  never from a baked constant. CNTFRQ on this board is 19.2 MHz against
;  the Pi 4's 54 MHz - a board that baked the Pi 4's number would be wrong
;  here by a factor of 2.8, and every duration the payload measured would
;  be wrong by that factor while looking perfectly plausible.
;
;  THE DIVIDE IS DONE ON THE TICKS AND NOT ON THE PRODUCT. ticks * 1000000
;  overflows a signed 64-bit value after about fifteen minutes at 19.2 MHz,
;  which is exactly long enough for a bench session to look fine and a
;  truck to not. Dividing first costs sub-microsecond resolution, which no
;  caller of this seam has ever needed, and keeps the answer monotonic for
;  the life of the board.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE COUNTER IS READ ONCE INTO A LOCAL, and that is not tidiness: two
;  reads of a free-running counter in one expression are two different
;  numbers, and the whole-seconds term and the remainder term would then
;  disagree - producing an answer that occasionally goes BACKWARDS by up
;  to a second. A monotonic clock that is monotonic almost always is not
;  a monotonic clock, and the ABI promises this one never goes backwards.
Procedure.i HwMicros()
  Define hz.i
  Define t.i
  Define whole.i
  hz = TickHz()
  If hz <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  t = Ticks()
  whole = t / hz
  ProcedureReturn whole * 1000000 + ((t - whole * hz) * 1000000) / hz
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwPet() - pet the deadman.
;
;  A NO-OP HERE, AND IT IS AN HONEST ONE RATHER THAN A MISSING FEATURE.
;  There is no deadman on this board: Anvil is a UEFI application, the
;  firmware owns the PMIC and the watchdog, and `deadman` is not a command
;  the Q offers. A payload that pets in its own loop therefore costs one
;  call and nothing happens, which is the correct behaviour - the
;  alternative is a payload that has to know which board it is on in order
;  to know whether to pet, and that is the fork the whole capability model
;  exists to prevent.
;
;  IT IS NOT GATED BY A #CAP_ FLAG for the same reason HwReboot is not:
;  the payload does not branch on this, it just calls it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwPet()
EndProcedure
